<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/advanced/language-and-meaning">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the capstone of the analytic line. It builds on the foundational
    <em>Logic: The Shape of an Argument</em>, <em>Epistemology: What Can We Know?</em> and
    <em>Metaphysics: What Is Real?</em> — every argument you tested there arrived as a sentence — and on the
    intermediate <em>Philosophy of Mind</em> and <em>Philosophy of Science</em>.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state Frege's distinction between sense and reference, and use it to explain why some identity statements are discoveries;</li>
    <li>apply Russell's analysis to a sentence containing a description that names nothing, and track the scope of a negation;</li>
    <li>contrast early and late Wittgenstein on meaning, and say what the private language argument is meant to show;</li>
    <li>analyse an utterance as a speech act with locutionary content, illocutionary force and felicity conditions;</li>
    <li>run the verificationist, falsificationist and Wittgensteinian readings of a religious sentence and say what each commits you to.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy of language was the engine room of twentieth-century philosophy: Frege, Russell, Wittgenstein,
    Quine, Austin, Grice and Kripke each built their systems on how words mean, because every other philosophical
    question — about God, mind, science or ethics — arrives as a <em>sentence</em>. If we do not understand how
    sentences mean, we do not fully understand what we are asking. This lesson covers the core machinery
    (reference, descriptions, use, speech acts) and then applies it to the tradition's hardest stress test: the
    <strong>religious language</strong> problem — whether sentences like "God exists" or "God is good" are
    truth-apt claims at all, and what it would take to verify or falsify them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="sense, reference, use — and the speech acts hiding in plain speech" widget="equationStep" config={langCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards and watch the definition of "meaning"
    shift under your feet — from Frege's relation between word and object, to Russell's logical repair of grammar,
    to Wittgenstein's public use, to Austin's acts. Each card keeps the previous machinery but relocates where
    meaning lives: in the proposition, in logical form, in the practice, in the doing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> philosophy of language is the discipline's engine room because every other question
    reaches us as a sentence. We start with Frege's puzzle about two names for Venus.
  </p>

  <h2>Sense and reference</h2>
  <p>
    Frege's 1892 paper begins with a puzzle: "the morning star is the evening star" is informative, yet
    "the morning star is the morning star" is trivial — although both name the same planet, Venus. If meaning
    were just the object named, the two sentences would mean the same, and astronomy could never have taught
    anyone anything. Frege's solution separates <strong>reference</strong> (the thing picked out) from
    <strong>sense</strong> (the mode of presentation): "morning star" and "evening star" present Venus under
    different descriptions, which is why their identity was a discovery. The distinction explains more than
    identity statements. It explains how a name can be meaningful with no existing bearer ("Odysseus" has sense
    but, arguably, no reference), and it grounds the compositionality principle that made formal semantics
    possible: the meaning of a whole is built from the senses of its parts.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each Fregean term with its work" widget="match" config={langFregeMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if two expressions pick out the same thing, they mean the same thing — so
  'the morning star is the evening star' says no more than 'the morning star is the morning star'." It seems
  reasonable because both names terminate at the same planet. But the sentences differ exactly in what Frege
  called sense, the mode of presentation, and that difference is why the first was an astronomical discovery
  while the second is trivial. The correct picture: reference fixes <em>what</em> a term points at; sense fixes
  <em>how</em> it is given, and only sense explains informativeness. "Odysseus" shows the two can even come
  apart — a term may have sense and, arguably, no reference at all.</p>

  <h2>Descriptions: Russell's logical surgery</h2>
  <p>
    Russell's 1905 "On Denoting" performs the method at its sharpest. "The present king of France is bald" is a
    problem: the phrase "the present king of France" names nothing, so is the sentence false, meaningless, or
    true of nothing? Russell's analysis rewrites the surface grammar into quantified logic: "there exists
    exactly one present king of France, and whatever is a present king of France is bald." Now the sentence is
    simply <em>false</em> — because its first conjunct fails — and the negation, "the present king of France is
    not bald," is true (there is no such person to be bald). Two different readings, once invisible, are
    separated by the scope of the negation. The deeper lesson is methodological: <strong>surface grammar
    systematically misleads about logical form</strong>, and philosophy's job is to expose the bones beneath.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the faulty line — the Russell gloss" widget="spot-error" config={langRussellSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Russell's theory is not the claim that names are shorthand for
  descriptions of the speaker's beliefs. Russell analysed definite <em>descriptions</em> ("the present king")
  into quantifiers; whether ordinary proper names are disguised descriptions is a separate, contested thesis —
  and the one Kripke attacked.</p>

  <h2>Wittgenstein twice: picture, then use</h2>
  <p>
    Early Wittgenstein (the <em>Tractatus</em>, 1921) held that a meaningful proposition is a <strong>picture
    of a possible state of affairs</strong>: language mirrors the logical structure of the world, and the
    meaningful is exhausted by what can be depicted. Ethics, aesthetics and the mystical lie outside the world
    of facts, hence the famous closing: "whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent." Late Wittgenstein
    (the <em>Philosophical Investigations</em>, 1953) dismantled his earlier self. Meaning, he now argued, is
    <strong>use within a language game</strong> — a social practice with rules and purposes. Words do not all
    work the same way: "game" itself has no single essence, only overlapping similarities he called
    <strong>family resemblance</strong>. Meaning is not an inner picture but a public skill, like following a
    rule, and the <strong>private language argument</strong> presses the point: a language that only one person
    could in principle understand — whose words refer to inner episodes checkable by no one — could not fix
    meanings at all, because there would be no difference between getting it right and seeming to. Philosophy,
    on this view, is therapy: dissolving problems that arise when "language goes on holiday."
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each claim — early or late Wittgenstein" widget="sort-buckets" config={langWittSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> meaning as picture, then meaning as use — and the use view comes with teeth: without
    a public standard of correctness there is no meaning at all. Austin now shows what words do when they are not
    describing anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Speech acts: doing things with words</h2>
  <p>
    Austin's <em>How to Do Things with Words</em> (1962) noticed a class of sentences logicians had mis-filed:
    "I promise to repay you," "I name this ship Liberté," "I now pronounce you married." These are not
    <em>descriptions</em> that are true or false; they are <strong>performatives</strong> — utterances that
    perform acts. Austin's analysis generalises: every utterance has a <strong>locutionary</strong> content
    (what is said), an <strong>illocutionary</strong> force (what is done in saying it — asserting, warning,
    promising, ordering), and a <strong>perlocutionary</strong> effect (what it causes — frightening,
    convincing). Speech acts come with <strong>felicity conditions</strong>: the ship-naming fails if the
    speaker lacks authority, just as a promise fails if made to a doorknob. The framework quietly explains a
    great deal of institutional life — contracts, laws, apologies, verdicts are all felicity-governed speech
    acts — and later speech-act theory (Searle) made explicit the rules constituting each act.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="fill the blanks — the anatomy of a speech act" widget="cloze" config={langSpeechCloze} />

  <h2>Reference without descriptions — and Quine's grenade</h2>
  <p>
    Kripke's <em>Naming and Necessity</em> (1972) overturned the descriptivist orthodoxy. Names, he argued,
    do not abbreviate descriptions in the speaker's head: "Aristotle" refers through a
    <strong>causal-historical chain</strong> running from your use back to the man himself, and it refers even
    if <em>every</em> description you associate with the name is false (Aristotle might have been none of the
    things we believe). The payoff is a new category, the <strong>necessary a posteriori</strong>: "water is
    H₂O" was discovered by chemistry, yet once true it is true in every possible world with water — necessity
    and knowability come apart. Quine had meanwhile thrown "Two Dogmas of Empiricism" (1951) at the analytic/
    synthetic distinction itself, arguing that our statements face experience as a corporate web and no
    sentence is immune to revision — meaning became holistic, and the most destabilising paper of the century
    forced everyone to say what they meant by "meaning." Grice then added the layer logic cannot see:
    <strong>implicature</strong>. "Can you pass the salt?" is a request, not a grammar query, and conversation
    runs on cooperative maxims (quantity, quality, relation, manner) whose violations are themselves
    informative. Pragmatics — the linguistics ladder's branch on this same tree — grew from exactly here.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — chains, webs and maxims" widget="judge" config={langKripkeJudge} />

  <h2>The hardest case: religious language</h2>
  <p>
    Now apply the machinery. <strong>Cognitivists</strong> hold that "God exists" and "God is good" aim at
    truth — they assert something and are true or false. <strong>Non-cognitivists</strong> hold that they do
    something else: express attitudes, commitments or stances (in the way "hurray!" expresses without
    describing). The twentieth century's great weapon for the non-cognitivist side was the
    <strong>verification principle</strong>: A. J. Ayer's <em>Language, Truth and Logic</em> (1936) declared a
    statement meaningful only if analytic (true by definition) or empirically verifiable in principle. Religious
    claims are neither, so they are not false but <em>literally meaningless</em> — expressions of emotion,
    like poetry's. The principle is elegant and notoriously self-undermining: it is itself neither analytic nor
    empirically verifiable, so on its own test it fails. Most philosophers now treat verificationism as too
    strong, but the dialectic it generated is the syllabus's University Debate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Antony Flew's <strong>falsification challenge</strong> (1950) sharpened the attack: a claim that admits no
    imaginable state of affairs that would count <em>against</em> it "dies the death of a thousand
    qualifications." His parable: two explorers find a clearing; one says a gardener tends it, the other
    denies it. Every proposed test — scent, footprints, electric fence — is met with a qualification ("a
    gardener invisible to any test") until the assertion says nothing. John Hick's famous response is
    <strong>eschatological verification</strong>: religious claims do entail checkable consequences, just at the
    end of an afterlife — verifiable, if true, in principle, so they pass the weak form of the principle.
    R. M. Hare countered with <strong>bliks</strong>: some commitments are not claims about the world at all but
    fundamental stances that colour every interpretation of it (his paranoid student who "knows" dons plan to
    murder him is not wrong about evidence — he has a corrupted blik). Religious faith, Hare suggested, is a
    blik, and bliks are not the kind of thing verification touches. Basil Mitchell's <em>partisan</em>
    occupies the middle: the resistance fighter who trusts his leader's coded instructions even when they look
    like betrayal <em>is</em> making a commitment that evidence against cannot simply cancel — faith as trust
    that acknowledges contrary evidence but holds anyway. And the Wittgensteinian wing dissolves the frame:
    religious language is its own language game, governed by its own criteria of use ("God exists" functions
    more like a vow or an orientation to life than a meteorological report), and judging it by scientific
    standards is a category error.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="walk the century's readings of one sentence" widget="branch" config={langGodBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the verification principle proved religious claims are false." It proved
  nothing of the sort: Ayer's principle classes religious sentences as neither true nor false — as meaningless,
  like expressions of emotion. And the principle never even got that far on its own terms, since it is itself
  neither analytic nor empirically verifiable and so fails its own test. It seems reasonable to remember it as
  an attack because it was aimed at religion's central claims; the correct picture is a self-undermining
  proposal that set the debate's terms — which is why Hick, Hare and Mitchell show religious language passing
  weaker tests or not being a truth-claim at all, not concede falsehood.</p>

  <h2>The hardest case, worked: one sentence, five readings</h2>
  <p>
    Watch the whole toolkit converge on a single religious sentence — "God is love." The
    <strong>cognitivist</strong> reads it as a proposition: true or false depending on God's nature — and
    immediately meets the verification problem (what observation would test it?). Ayer's
    <strong>verificationist</strong> rules it meaningless; Hick's eschatological move makes it verifiable
    at the end of things; Flew's falsificationist demands a state of affairs that would count against it
    and hears only qualifications. Hare's <strong>blik</strong> reading says the sentence does not report
    but <em>orient</em> — it states the stance from which all evidence is read, so calling it "false" is a
    category mistake. The <strong>Wittgensteinian</strong> asks about use: within the religious language
    game the sentence functions as commitment and self-definition (closer to "I am loyal to this" than to
    "water boils at 100°"), and its intelligibility is settled by its role in the practice. The
    <strong>speech-act</strong> reading finds illocutionary force: a vow or avowal whose felicity
    conditions concern the speaker's sincerity, not the world's furniture. And the Fregean
    <strong>sense/reference</strong> question undercuts them all: what, if anything, does "God" refer to,
    and can a term with contested reference enter truth-apt sentences at all? Five centuries of debate,
    one sentence — which is why philosophy of language is the load-bearing course for philosophy of
    religion.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each reading with its verdict on 'God is love'" widget="match" config={langFiveMatch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is "the morning star is the evening star" informative while "the morning star is the morning star"
    is trivial, given that both name Venus?</li>
    <li>Under Russell's analysis, what exactly does "the present king of France is not bald" claim, and what
    makes its negation come out true?</li>
    <li>Ayer's verification principle declares "God is good" meaningless. Give two reasons most philosophers
    refuse to accept that verdict.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The sentences differ in sense — the mode of presentation — though their
    reference is identical; identity statements taught astronomers something because Venus is given one way as a
    dawn object and another as a dusk object. (2) Russell rewrites it into quantified logic: there exists exactly
    one present king of France, and whatever is such a king is not bald. As a whole it is false — the existence
    clause fails — so its negation, "the present king of France is bald", comes out true: two readings separated
    by the scope of the negation. (3) First, the principle fails its own test: it is neither analytic nor
    empirically verifiable. Second, the debate it generated showed religious language slipping through weaker
    nets — Hick's eschatological verification, Hare's bliks — so "meaningless" was never the forced conclusion.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Frege splits meaning into sense (mode of presentation) and reference (the thing picked out) — the difference that makes identity informative.</li>
    <li>Russell analyses descriptions into quantifiers: surface grammar systematically misleads about logical form.</li>
    <li>Early Wittgenstein: a proposition pictures a possible state of affairs. Late Wittgenstein: meaning is use within a language game, policed by public standards.</li>
    <li>Austin's speech acts: every utterance has locutionary content, illocutionary force and felicity conditions — words do things.</li>
    <li>Kripke's causal-historical chains replace descriptivism for names and ground the necessary a posteriori.</li>
    <li>The religious-language debate runs five rival readings of one sentence — machinery, not eloquence, decides.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Modal Logic</em> pushes the same formalising instinct from sentences to what must and might be the case —
    and states the arguments for God's existence, the sibling of this lesson's religious-language debate, at full
    strength. <em>Phenomenology &amp; Existentialism</em> is the continental counter-current: where this lesson
    analyses sentences, it analyses lived experience — reading the two together shows what each tradition cannot
    see in the other.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three currents run beneath the surface you have now seen. <strong>The rule-following paradox</strong>
    (Investigations §201, and Kripke's 1982 reconstruction): no fact about my past behaviour can determine what
    I <em>mean</em> by "plus" rather than a deviant function that agrees on every case I have computed — so
    rule-following seems to rest on the community, not the private mind, with destabilising consequences for
    semantics and the philosophy of mind. <strong>Theories of truth</strong> hide inside every account of
    meaning: correspondence (Frege-Russell era), coherence, and Tarski's formal definition, which Davidson
    parlayed into a meaning-theory — a T-sentence for each sentence of a language. <strong>Speech acts meet
    institutions and machines</strong>: Searle's "constitutive rules" (X counts as Y in context C) formalise
    money, marriage and law as giant speech-act structures, and the question of whether large language models
    — systems that produce grammatical assertions without lives to refer from — can perform genuine assertions
    is now a live research debate in the philosophy of AI.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open questions are the tradition's oldest: does reference need a thinker with a world (the
    externalism/internalism debate after Putnam's twin earth), can non-cognitivism avoid explaining away
    believers' sincere claims that they are stating facts, and is "meaning as use" a theory at all or the
    refusal of one? Notice how far you have come: the tools that settled puzzles about Venus and bald kings are
    the same tools deciding whether entire domains of human discourse are coherent.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Frege (1892); Russell (1905); Wittgenstein (1921, 1953); Kripke, <em>Naming and Necessity</em> (1972); Austin, <em>How to Do Things with Words</em> (1962); Ayer (1936); Flew, Hare &amp; Mitchell, "Theology and Falsification" (1950–55); Hick, "Theology and Verification" (1963). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
